Speed up LibreDWG's handle reference index (src/dwg.c).

dwg_add_handleref() looks up existing refs in a sorted index. Two problems
made adding many entities quadratic:

1. Ref_cmp returned 1 for both "greater" and "less", so the index was never
   properly sorted, and the first duplicate key disabled it for good, falling
   back to a linear scan over every ref on each lookup.
2. Sorting by handle code first meant most new refs were inserted in front of
   existing ones, shifting the whole array.

Now refs are ordered by (absolute handle, code), duplicates keep the first
ref (matching the linear search), and new refs append at the end.
Adding 60,000 3DFACEs goes from minutes to well under a second.

--- a/src/dwg.c
+++ b/src/dwg.c
@@ -3871,12 +3871,12 @@
 static int
 Ref_cmp (const Dwg_Object_Ref *pKey, const Dwg_Object_Ref **ppR)
 {
-  int retVal = (int)pKey->handleref.code - (int)(*ppR)->handleref.code;
-  if (0 != retVal)
-    return retVal;
-  return pKey->absolute_ref > (*ppR)->absolute_ref    ? 1
-         : pKey->absolute_ref == (*ppR)->absolute_ref ? 0
-                                                      : 1;
+  // Order by absref first: new handles only grow, so adding refs appends
+  // instead of shifting the whole array. (The old code also returned 1 for
+  // "less", which is not a valid ordering for bsearch.)
+  if (pKey->absolute_ref != (*ppR)->absolute_ref)
+    return pKey->absolute_ref > (*ppR)->absolute_ref ? 1 : -1;
+  return (int)pKey->handleref.code - (int)(*ppR)->handleref.code;
 }
 
 void
@@ -3952,11 +3952,9 @@
     }
   else
     {
-      // There are duplicates, disable bsearch
-      // It should never happen !
-      free (dwg->object_ordered_ref);
-      dwg->object_ordered_ref = NULL;
-      dwg->num_object_ordered_refs = -1;
+      // Duplicate code+absref (e.g. entity refs added without a search).
+      // Keep the first one, which is what the linear search would return,
+      // instead of disabling bsearch and making every later lookup O(n).
     }
 }
 
